      "ballot_id": "B007",
      "proposed_laureates": ["Harald Rose", "Knut Urban", "Maximilian Haider"],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "Spherical aberration correction made atomic-scale electron microscopy broadly practical, changing how materials structure and defects can be directly observed.",
        "attribution": "Rose provided key optical theory, while Haider and Urban were central to realizing and demonstrating effective correctors. The three-part credit reflects distinct, complementary contributions.",
        "maturity": "The instruments have enabled decades of high-resolution work across materials science, with extensive independent use and validation.",
        "uncertainties": ["The relative weight of the theoretical proposal, instrument engineering, and early applications is difficult to divide precisely."]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 2,
      "ballot_id": "B011",
      "proposed_laureates": ["Antoine Georges", "Dieter Vollhardt", "Gabriel Kotliar"],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "Dynamical mean-field theory gave condensed-matter physics a powerful framework for correlated electrons, especially the Mott transition, and remains widely used.",
        "attribution": "Georges, Kotliar, and Vollhardt made central and complementary contributions to establishing and developing the framework.",
        "maturity": "The approach has been extensively tested and extended over several decades, including in quantitative comparisons with experiments and materials calculations.",
        "uncertainties": ["The work grew through several groups and later extensions, so a three-person attribution may not capture every foundational contribution."]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 3,
      "ballot_id": "B052",
      "proposed_laureates": ["Charles L. Kane", "Eugene J. Mele", "Laurens W. Molenkamp"],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "The prediction and experimental realization of topological insulators and the quantum spin Hall effect established a major new class of quantum materials and transport phenomena.",
        "attribution": "Kane and Mele supplied foundational theoretical predictions; Molenkamp's experiments provided decisive evidence for the quantum spin Hall state.",
        "maturity": "The predictions and experimental results have been followed by extensive confirmation and a broad body of work on topological materials.",
        "uncertainties": ["The experimental demonstrations relied on particular material systems, and credit for the wider field extends beyond these foundational contributions."]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 4,
      "ballot_id": "B038",
      "proposed_laureates": ["Jagadeesh S. Moodera", "Stuart S. P. Parkin", "Terunobu Miyazaki"],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "Large room-temperature tunnel magnetoresistance in MgO junctions linked a striking quantum transport effect to practical magnetic memory and sensing technologies.",
        "attribution": "The credited researchers led important, partly parallel experimental advances in high-performance magnetic tunnel junctions.",
        "maturity": "The effect has been replicated, understood in substantial detail, and incorporated into widely used device technologies.",
        "uncertainties": ["The discoveries were made in close succession by multiple groups, making the division of priority and individual credit complex."]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 5,
      "ballot_id": "B044",
      "proposed_laureates": ["Qi-Kun Xue", "Xi Dai", "Zhong Fang"],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "The quantum anomalous Hall effect demonstrated quantized transport without an applied magnetic field and gave topological band theory a striking materials realization.",
        "attribution": "Xue's group achieved the landmark experimental observation, while Dai and Fang made important theoretical contributions to the material platform and its interpretation.",
        "maturity": "The effect has been confirmed and developed further, though experimental conditions and material performance remain demanding.",
        "uncertainties": ["The balance of credit between the decisive experiment and theory, as well as the role of other contributors to the material system, needs careful evaluation."]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 6,
      "ballot_id": "B019",
      "proposed_laureates": ["Allan H. MacDonald", "James P. Eisenstein", "Loren N. Pfeiffer"],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "Exciton condensation in quantum Hall bilayers revealed coherent many-body behavior in a clean, controllable low-dimensional system.",
        "attribution": "MacDonald's theoretical work and the experimental advances by Eisenstein and Pfeiffer form a persuasive theory-experiment account of the discovery.",
        "maturity": "The phenomenon has been studied over many years, with multiple signatures supporting the interpretation.",
        "uncertainties": ["The precise scope of the condensate phase and interpretation of some transport signatures remain active subjects of research."]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 7,
      "ballot_id": "B003",
      "proposed_laureates": ["Michel Della Negra", "Peter Jenni", "Tejinder Virdee"],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "The ATLAS and CMS discovery of the Higgs boson was a landmark confirmation of the electroweak theory and required exceptional experimental coordination.",
        "attribution": "These leaders were central to building and guiding the two experiments, but the result depended on very large collaborations and many essential contributions.",
        "maturity": "The discovery has been confirmed through extensive measurements of the particle's properties.",
        "uncertainties": ["Credit for a collaboration-scale discovery cannot be represented fully by three individuals, and the proposed attribution needs to reflect that limitation."]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 8,
      "ballot_id": "B049",
      "proposed_laureates": ["Allan H. MacDonald", "Pablo Jarillo-Herrero", "Rafi Bistritzer"],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "Moiré flat bands in twisted bilayer graphene opened a highly tunable platform for studying correlated insulating and superconducting states.",
        "attribution": "Bistritzer and MacDonald developed the influential theoretical description, and Jarillo-Herrero's group found the key correlated phenomena experimentally.",
        "maturity": "The field has produced extensive follow-up work, but the relationship among flat bands, superconductivity, and other competing phases is still being resolved.",
        "uncertainties": ["The physical mechanisms and generality of the superconducting state remain unsettled, and priority across the rapidly developing field is distributed."]
      }
    }
  ]
